WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.339 --> 00:00:09.820
Classroom technology has prompted dramatic
promises that computers will revolutionize

00:00:09.820 --> 00:00:13.340
learning, and dire warnings of what might
ensue.

00:00:13.340 --> 00:00:18.420
What both camps share is the tendency to take
the march of technology as a given.

00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:24.180
But in an unexpected twist, a recent international
analysis shows that elementary students used

00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:28.230
less classroom technology in 2016 than a decade
before.

00:00:28.230 --> 00:00:34.660
In 2006, three-quarters of U.S. 4th graders
said they “used computers at school at least

00:00:34.660 --> 00:00:35.859
once a week.”

00:00:35.860 --> 00:00:39.080
By 2016, that figure had declined markedly.

00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:42.240
What this means isn’t entirely
clear.

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:45.720
Are students simply using phones instead of
computers?

00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:49.560
Has the meaning of “use a computer” simply
changed over time?

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:53.120
Or, are schools moving to restrict the use
of technology?

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:55.050
That’s all up for debate.

00:00:55.050 --> 00:00:56.530
My takeaway?

00:00:56.530 --> 00:01:01.680
That it’s always useful to check our grand
education narratives against the prosaic facts.

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:07.440
And that the facts themselves aren’t always
as straightforward as we  might wish.

00:01:08.540 --> 00:01:12.760
Do you think students are using less technology today than they were a decade ago?

